{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are generally below realized prices, especially from ~day 6 to ~day 25. Apply an upward level shift (≈ +0.5 to +1.5 €/t on average; larger when market is trending up).",
    "Underreaction to upside momentum/spikes: after sharp rises, the model lags and misses the continuation (true path stays elevated). Add a momentum term: if last 5–10 days slope is positive, lift the next 1–15 day forecast more aggressively.",
    "Excessive mean-reversion in mid-horizon: the model pulls prices back toward the prior mean too quickly, while outcomes remain in a higher regime. Reduce mean-reversion strength for horizons ~7–20 days when recent volatility/trend is high.",
    "Late-horizon direction errors: the model often projects declines or extreme moves at days ~25–30 that do not materialize (either too low or too high). Damp late-horizon changes by shrinking day 25–30 deviations toward the day 15–20 level (e.g., 30–50% shrinkage).",
    "Volatility miscalibration: forecast path is too smooth early/mid horizon (misses higher realized variability) but can become too erratic at the tail. Increase short-horizon variance (days 1–10) slightly; cap tail volatility (days 20–30) with a max daily change constraint."
  ]
}
